    description: "[REVIEW 2026-11-01, CURRENT] Review 2026-11-01 at dev 100b6fbb6e: the item's DONE-WHEN fixture reads inconclusive on host_secret->logging and ->host_fs ('variadic_call at main.c:13'); main->log_print carries unfollowed_construct=variadic_call and vfprintf is an unclassified external (no c.yaml row). Premise correction: fprintf is rowed fs_write (zone host_fs) and stderr is only a logging attribute, so a direct fprintf(stderr,..) control reads host_fs violated but logging confirmed; the DONE-WHEN claim must be host_secret->host_fs (or the stderr->logging split decided first). (Original text below; the review entry in the thread has the evidence.)\n\nTrackerized from INV-nohob (owner, 2026-10-19). One of four constructs C taint does not follow; INV-nohob's disclosure withholds the clean verdict meanwhile.\n\nWHAT. A tainted argument passed in a C project function's VARIADIC tail (`...`) does not reach a sink the function reaches through va_list. torsocks' log_print(const char *fmt, ...) (src/common/log.c) va_starts an ap and writes it with a vfprintf-family call to the log file / stderr; the password DBG passes arrives in the variadic tail. c_def_use does not connect the `...` parameter to `va_list ap` / va_start(ap, fmt), so the interprocedural walk has no parameter to carry the taint to.\n\nFIX DIRECTION (not designed): treat a variadic parameter as one pseudo-parameter that every argument at or after its position maps to; va_start(ap, last) defines ap from it; the v*printf family (vfprintf, vsnprintf, vprintf, vsyslog ...) uses ap as a data argument. Check the catalogue rows for the v* family exist and carry the right boundary.\n\nDONE WHEN: a fixture `void log_print(const char *fmt, ...) { va_list ap; va_start(ap, fmt); vfprintf(stderr, fmt, ap); va_end(ap); }` + log_print(\"%s\", getenv(\"PASS\")) reads violated on a host_secret -> logging claim. With the macro item, torsocks' host-secret-no-logging should then read violated. When it lands, narrow INV-nohob's disclosure for varargs."  # 85c1
